---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="This site's own infrastructure — Somasuntaram">
  <h1>This site's own infrastructure</h1>

  <p>
    Static site, but the infra decisions weren't trivial — each one has a
    reason, and I can defend all of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Why static + CloudFront over anything server-rendered</h2>
  <p>
    Content is entirely author-controlled — there's no user data, no auth,
    no dynamic query path. A server introduces attack surface and cost for
    zero functional gain. Astro builds to static HTML; CloudFront + S3 gives
    global edge caching without running compute I'd have to patch and
    monitor.
  </p>

  <h2>S3 access model</h2>
  <p>
    The bucket blocks all public access. CloudFront reaches it through an
    Origin Access Control (OAC) — the modern replacement for the older OAI
    pattern — and the bucket policy is scoped to this distribution's exact
    ARN via a <code>StringEquals</code> condition on <code>AWS:SourceArn</code>,
    not a wildcard CloudFront service principal. That distinction matters:
    a wildcard principal would let <em>any</em> CloudFront distribution in
    the account read the bucket, not just this one.
  </p>

  <h2>The state backend bootstrap problem</h2>
  <p>
    Terraform's S3 backend can't create its own state bucket — the storage
    has to exist before Terraform can point at it. I bootstrap it once via
    AWS CLI (bucket + versioning + a DynamoDB table for state locking), then
    hand off to Terraform for everything downstream. The alternative — a
    separate bootstrap Terraform root with local state — is more "correct"
    for a team but is overkill for a single-stack personal site. Worth
    saying explicitly if asked, rather than pretending everything is
    Terraform-managed end to end.
  </p>

  <h2>ACM cross-region requirement</h2>
  <p>
    CloudFront only accepts ACM certificates issued in <code>us-east-1</code>,
    regardless of which region the rest of the stack lives in. The Terraform
    config uses a provider alias so the primary provider stays in
    <code>ap-south-1</code> for anything region-flexible while the cert
    resource is pinned to <code>us-east-1</code> explicitly, rather than
    quietly running the whole stack out of Virginia.
  </p>

  <h2>Deploy pipeline</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub Actions builds the Astro site, syncs the output to S3, then
    issues a CloudFront invalidation for changed paths. No blue/green
    needed — it's static assets behind a CDN, so cache invalidation is the
    entire deployment problem. The interesting failure mode here is a stale
    invalidation scope (invalidating <code>/*</code> on every deploy is
    correct but costs more; targeted invalidation is cheaper but risks
    missing a changed shared asset) — this stack takes the simpler
    <code>/*</code> approach deliberately, given the site's low deploy
    frequency and file count.
  </p>

  <h2>What I'd change at higher traffic</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing here is designed for scale because it doesn't need to be. If
    this were a real product surface: WAF in front of CloudFront, a
    separate staging distribution, and probably moving off
    <code>PriceClass_100</code> if traffic showed meaningful non-NA/EU
    demand. Saying "this would need X at scale" without X actually being
    load-bearing anywhere is a smell I'm avoiding here.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
